;; Leibniz: the characteristic triangle and transmutation (Paris, 1673-1674)
;; and the rules of Nova Methodus pro Maximis et Minimis (Acta Eruditorum,
;; October 1684). English from J. M. Child, The Early Mathematical
;; Manuscripts of Leibniz (Open Court, 1920); the Latin of 1684 as reprinted
;; in Dutens, Leibnitii Opera Omnia, vol. III (Geneva, 1768), pp. 167-172,
;; with the series' own English rendering, marked as such.
;;
;; The rules are claims over the vocabulary's `differential` (Leibniz's d;
;; alexandria.vocab realizes it through Emmy's D). Leibniz's constant a is
;; declared in the board's :constants.
{:tradition {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus
             :author "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz"
             :work "Nova Methodus pro Maximis et Minimis"
             :date "1684"
             :source "Acta Eruditorum, Leipzig, October 1684, pp. 467-473"}
 :propositions
 [{:id :leibniz/transmutation
   :title "The characteristic triangle and the transmutation of figures (1673)"
   :statement "However I, as soon as I had found a certain very general theorem, by means of which any figure whatever could be converted into another that is quite different from it, but yet of equivalent area, set to work to try whether the circle could not be converted in some way into a rational figure; and the thing came out beautifully."
   :source "Child 1920, p. 188"
   :work "Historia et origo (Child's translation of Gerhardt's text)"
   :claims [{:claim (= (* 2 (differential (* 1/2 x y))) (+ (* x dy) (* y dx)))
                            :label "twice the triangle's difference is x dy + y dx"}]
   :construction {:id :leibniz/transmutation :kind :construction :points [] :checks []}}
  {:id :leibniz/arithmetical-quadrature
   :title "The arithmetical quadrature of the circle"
   :statement "From which, by the help of ordinary geometry, it can be easily deduced that the square on the diameter is to the area of the circle as 1 is to 1/1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + etc."
   :source "Child 1920, pp. 189-190"
   :construction {:id :leibniz/arithmetical-quadrature :kind :construction :points [] :checks []}}
  {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus-product
   :title "Multiplicatio: d(xv) = x dv + v dx"
   :statement "Multiplicatio, d xv aequ. x dv + v dx, seu posito y aequ. xv, fiet dy aequ. x dv + v dx. [Series rendering: Multiplication: d(xv) equals x dv + v dx; or, putting y equal to xv, dy equals x dv + v dx.]"
   :source "Acta Eruditorum 1684, p. 467 (Dutens III, p. 167)"
   :claims [{:claim (= (differential (* x v)) (+ (* x dv) (* v dx)))}]
   :construction {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus-product :kind :construction :points [] :checks []}}
  {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus-quotient
   :title "Divisio: d(v/y) = (y dv - v dy)/yy"
   :statement "Porro Divisio, d v/y vel (posito z aequ. v/y) dz aequ. (±v dy ∓ y dv)/yy. [Series rendering: Next, division: d(v/y), or (putting z equal to v/y) dz, equals (±v dy ∓ y dv)/yy, the ambiguous signs read so that the fraction increases with x.]"
   :source "Acta Eruditorum 1684, p. 468 (Dutens III, p. 167)"
   :claims [{:claim (= (differential (/ v y)) (/ (- (* y dv) (* v dy)) (* y y)))}]
   :construction {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus-quotient :kind :construction :points [] :checks []}}
  {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus-power
   :title "Potentia: d(x^a) = a x^(a-1) dx"
   :statement "Potentia d x^a = a x^(a-1) dx, exempli gratia d x^3 = 3 x^2 dx. [Series rendering: Power: d of x to the a equals a x^(a-1) dx; for example, d x^3 = 3 x^2 dx.]"
   :source "Acta Eruditorum 1684, p. 469 (Dutens III, p. 168)"
   :claims [{:claim (= (differential (expt x 3)) (* 3 (expt x 2) dx))}]
   :construction {:id :leibniz/nova-methodus-power :kind :construction :points [] :checks []}}]}
